    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip. so if it's just the crystals separating, is it mostly a temperature issue or can it be caused by something else like how i'm storing it? basically just trying to understand why this 'bloom' keeps appearing even when i think i've done the cooling right.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While temperature is the primary driver, humidity during storage is the most common culprit for recurring bloom, as moisture released from humid air causes the cocoa butter migration you observed. If your bars are stored in a dry environment but the bloom persists, it likely indicates that your tempering cooled the surface too slowly, allowing an unstable crystal structure to form before the flavor transferred. You can test this by placing fresh batches in a sealed bag with a silica pack during cooling; if the flower appears with the silica but not without, your issue is definitely humidity-related.",
